```
FINDINGS:
1. [MINOR] src/repl/debug_repl.rs:369 — `take_reports()` uses `self.state.lock()` directly instead of the module's `lock()` helper (defined at line 388), which handles poisoned mutexes by recovering the inner state. If a watch evaluation panics and poisons the mutex, subsequent calls to `take_reports()` will return an empty vec instead of recovering the reports. All other lock acquisitions in the file (lines 124, 195, 211, 230, 325, 401) correctly use the `lock()` helper.
2. [MAJOR] src/repl/debug_repl.rs:115 — The REPORT.md documents that the scratch VM uses `ask_questions(true)` to refuse effectful builtins (`files.write`, `network.post`) during watch evaluation, but no test verifies this behavior. This is an advertised safety feature without test coverage (edge-case gap per AGENTS.md §3.2). A test should assert that `.watch files.write("x", "y")` reports a refusal rather than performing the write.
3. [STYLE] src/repl/debug_repl.rs:587 — `names_a_file()` has a TOCTOU race: `std::path::Path::new(argument).is_file()` checks existence, but the file could be deleted before `read_source()` reads it. Acceptable for a REPL debugging tool but a known anti-pattern.

REVIEW VERDICT: FINDINGS 3
```
